    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d386">

                <pb facs="0546" n="450" xml:id="pg450"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">812.00/25080</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Chairman of the Pan-American Federation of Labor</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Gompers</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1921-07-08"><hi rendition="#italic">July 8,
                        1921</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have received your letter of July
                        7th<anchor n="64" xml:id="d386-f1"/> in which you quote a telegram received
                    from Mr. Davison, Secretary of the International Association of Machinists, and
                    Mr. John Kelly, who are now in Orizaba, Mexico, and request that a statement be
                    made that the sending of ships to Mexican waters is “not with the object of
                    interfering with the struggle of the workers in Tampico.”</p>
                <p>Permit me to say in reply, that the presence of the ships has nothing whatever to
                    do with labor unions to which Mr. Davison refers in his telegram or with
                    disputes between employers and employees, but is simply a precautionary measure
                    for the purpose of assuring adequate protection to the lives and property of
                    American citizens. It is hoped, of course, that this protection will be accorded
                    by the local authorities.</p>
                <p>I am [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Charles E. Hughes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
